      "side": 1,
      "message": "The similar feedback on line #140 is valid, however this instance is different.\n\nThis `const` does *not* shadow the imported type. Both names stay in scope, in the declaration space each one belongs to.\n\n`import type { Manager }` is a type-only name. It is erased at compile time and creates no runtime binding. `const Manager` is a value. TypeScript resolves them by position:\n\n* `computed\u003cManager | null\u003e`, and any later `: Manager` annotation, still refers to the imported interface.\n* `Manager.value` and the returned `Manager` refer to this const.\n\nShadowing would mean the inner binding hides the outer one, so a use of the name resolves to the inner binding instead. That does not happen on this line. If the const hid the type, Manager in `computed\u003cManager | null\u003e` would refer to the value being declared and the file would not typecheck. A value import of `Manager` in this same scope would be a duplicate-identifier error. The type-only import is what makes the pair legal. It is the same split TypeScript uses when one spelling is both a type and a value.\n\nThat shared spelling is the maintainability *gain*. The schema, the TypeScript type, the cached JSON, and this binding are all `Manager`, so a view reads `Manager.LastResetTime` and `Manager.Actions[\u0027#Manager.Reset\u0027]` with no rename step. Renaming the local to `manager` splits them: either callers learn a second name beside the schema, or the return becomes `{ Manager: manager }` and we maintain an alias. The alias is what has to change when Redfish changes. This binding does not.\n\nThis is the Naming rule in the Redfish-first data layer design (https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/webui-vue/+/94472): a local binding uses the Redfish type name (import type { Chassis } together with const Chassis), and review should leave that pair as written.\n\n@Aravinth S please review the Redfish-first design guide and feel free to provide feedback there (or give it a +1 if it is agreeable).",
